名词+이다 做谓语；\\n动词 + -는 것 把动作名词化；\\n形容词 + -:
Noun + 이다 as predicate;
Verb + -는 것 to nominalize actions;
Adjective + -게 to make adverbs of manner.
This lesson is Korean's "transformation toolkit"—letting verbs act as subjects and adjectives modify actions.
In Chinese, verbs can be subjects directly ("Learning is important"), but Korean requires -는 것 to nominalize the verb before it can sit in subject or object position.

三块内容：①명사+이에요（是……，已学基础）；\\n②-는 것（动作名词化，\":
Three parts: ① Noun + 이에요 ("is..."—already learned);
② -는 것 (nominalizing actions, "the act of studying");
③ -게 (adjectives to adverbs, "simply," "quickly").
The focus is on the last two—they make sentence structure more flexible.

에게서/한테서 表示\"从谁那里\"；\\n씨 是人名后的礼貌称呼；\\n짜리:
에게서/한테서 means "from someone"; 
씨 is a polite title after a person's name; 
짜리 indicates price/age/denomination. 
These three points are independent but all super practical in everyday Korean. 
Chinese uses one structure for "from a friend," but Korean distinguishes by register: 
에게서 (written) vs. 한테서 (spoken).

이/가 아니에요 表示\"不是\"；\\n이/가 아니라 B예요 表示\"不是A:
이/가 아니에요 means "is not"; 
이/가 아니라 B예요 means "it's not A but B"; 
-는 게 아니라 is used to correct an action. 
Correcting misunderstandings, clarifying facts, and contrasting two statements—this lesson's three structures are all for "setting the record straight." 
In Chinese, "not A but B" relies on word order and separate words, but Korean uses the dedicated structure 이/가 아니라 + B예요, and the particle choice depends on whether there's a final consonant.

三块内容：①이/가 아니에요（单纯否定\"不是\"）；\\n②이/가 아니라 B:
Three parts: ① 이/가 아니에요 (simple negation "is not"); 
② 이/가 아니라 B예요 (contrastive negation "not A but B"); 
③ -는 게 아니라 (action-level "not doing A"). 
아니라 must be followed by the correct answer; it can't be used alone.

三套冠词形：①动词现在→-는（공부하는 사람）；\\n②形容词→-은/ㄴ（按收音:
Three sets of adnominal forms: ① present verb → -는 (공부하는 사람); 
② adjective → -은/ㄴ (by final consonant, 어려운 문법); 
③ future/incomplete verb → -을/ㄹ (갈 곳). 
After conjugating, place directly before the noun—the structure is the same as Chinese "...的 + noun."
